### 27A. Operational evidence note

When execution proof rather than wording quality is the real issue:
- distinguish packet existence from operational evidence
- escalate from review memo to execution ledger, verdict sheet, controller audit packet, or promotion decision record only when that stronger artifact changes the decision quality
- if a weaker or stale compatible packet remains beside a stronger artifact, mark it superseded; if the required packet floor is missing, downgrade the claim before using higher-strength language
- keep `scenario_id`, `run_id`, `cohort_id`, `trace_id`, and `artifact_version` stable when benchmark, replay, adaptation, release, or telemetry packets must link across rounds
- if joined artifacts fail precedence, compatibility, freshness, or completeness checks, reject the merge or downgrade the claim instead of narrating a stronger result
- if stronger operational evidence is missing, downgrade the claim before using release-grade, drift-grade, or promotion-grade language
- keep `false-promotion`, `false-hold`, `drift-triggered review`, `rollback aftermath`, `route-switch failure`, `late clarification`, and `failed fallback timing` explicit when those failure surfaces explain why stronger proof is missing or downgraded

